# HOW MUCH OF THE SYSTEM THIS CORPUS CLAIMS TO HOLD. One row per extent a stack declares,
# counted over the documents that attest to a world.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE SAME SHAPE AS THE COUPLING SEARCH, ONE LEVEL UP, AND THE SAME TRAP. `scoped` says
#    somebody established what lies outside this stack and excluded it on purpose.
#    `unbounded` says nobody looked. Rendered as one boolean -- "is this stack complete?" --
#    a bounded selection and an unexamined one are the same row, and the difference is gone
#    for good. It is the coupling search's distinction applied to the stack itself.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE ANSWER NOBODY IN THIS CORPUS GIVES IS THE ONE WORTH NAMING. Not one filing
#    claims `complete`. A stack is a PROJECTION of one system, held by whoever filed it, and
#    reading it as an enumeration of the system is what turns a second document's extra layer
#    into an accusation that the first omitted something. The corpus declining to claim
#    completeness is the model's own reading, filed rather than asserted.
#
# ⛔ `complete` IS FALSIFIABLE IN PRINCIPLE AND BY NOTHING HERE, which is why this is a report
#   and not a rule. What would refute it is another filing holding a layer OF THE SAME SYSTEM
#   that this one lacks -- and no document names a system, only a filing.
-- pm:Stack/pm:scope over scope/corpus.sqlc; the extent axis is documented on that element.
SELECT CASE
         WHEN sc.extent = 'complete'  THEN 'the whole system is in this stack'
         WHEN sc.extent = 'scoped'    THEN 'a bounded selection: somebody said what is outside'
         WHEN sc.extent = 'unbounded' THEN 'NOBODY LOOKED at what lies outside'
         ELSE 'not stated: ' || sc.absent
       END      AS how_much_of_the_system,
       count(*) AS stacks
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/scopes.sqlc)
) sc
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
